{"text": "Liam learns most successfully through short priorities and realistic daily goals; flexible pacing during recovery; choice of quiet or supported classroom; and frequent feedback without public attention. These strategies make instruction clearer and reduce the amount of energy spent simply gaining access.\n\nThe profile also identifies predictable barriers. Liam may struggle with sleep disruption and fluctuating energy; sustained workload after absences; crowded, unpredictable settings; and stigma or disciplinary responses to illness. We should not treat these points as a list of personal deficits. Each one directs the team toward a response. For example, a barrier caused by pace may require additional processing time; an environmental barrier may require a quieter, safer, or more accessible setting; and task complexity may require modeling, chunking, direct instruction, or an alternate response.\n\nThe goal is not to remove every productive challenge. The goal is to remove barriers that prevent the student from engaging with the intended learning. Presenter 3 will now connect the profile’s sensory, medical, behavioural, and safety information to the EA’s day-to-day responsibilities.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-H-04.mp3", "words": [{"text": "Liam", "start": 0.1375, "end": 0.475, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 4}, {"text": "learns", "start": 0.4875, "end": 0.8375, "start_char": 5, "end_char": 11}, {"text": "most", "start": 0.85, "end": 1.1125, "start_char": 12, "end_char": 16}, {"text": "successfully", "start": 1.125, "end": 1.825, "start_char": 17, "end_char": 29}, {"text": "through", "start": 1.8375, "end": 2.0375, "start_char": 30, "end_char": 37}, {"text": "short", "start": 2.05, "end": 2.3625, "start_char": 38, "end_char": 43}, {"text": "priorities", "start": 2.375, "end": 3.1, "start_char": 44, "end_char": 54}, {"text": "and", "start": 3.1125, "end": 3.2875, "start_char": 55, "end_char": 58}, {"text": "realistic", "start":